XCOMM Atari System V Release 4 (UniSoft SVR4 for the TT030), cross-built on a
XCOMM Linux PC with gcc-cross-amix (the same ABI as AMIX). Derived from amix.cf.
XCOMM Stage 1: the Xatw server only, a static link over TCP.

#define OSMajorVersion 4
#define OSMinorVersion 0
#define OSTeenyVersion 0
#define OSName Atari System V Release 4.0
#define OSVendor "Atari"
#define OSDefines -DASV
#define OSRelease -DSVR4

#define SystemV4 YES

#define BootstrapCFlags -DASV_CROSS
XCOMM The compiler sees a fixincluded shadow of the sysroot (mksysroot.sh):
XCOMM ASV's headers assume AT&T cc -Xa (__STDC__ == 0), and stdio.h's m68k
XCOMM stream aliases break under GNU ld. m68k stays predefined, as the headers
XCOMM lay out regset/ucontext by it, so no -ansi.
#define StandardDefines OSDefines OSRelease
#define StandardCppDefines -undef -traditional OSDefines OSRelease

#define ProjectRoot /usr/x11r6
#define UsrLibDir /usr/x11r6/lib
#define LibDir /usr/x11r6/lib/X11
#define IncRoot /usr/x11r6/include
#define BinDir /usr/x11r6/bin
#define ManDirectoryRoot /usr/x11r6/man

#define HasGcc YES
#define HasGcc2 YES
#define HasGcc2ForCplusplus NO
XCOMM Shared X libraries with real SONAMEs (so no fixneeded for them), built
XCOMM -fPIC (-fpic calls through the PLT with 16-bit offsets, too short for
XCOMM libX11) and linked by GNU ld -shared; the static archives are kept for the
XCOMM server. Clients find them in /usr/x11r6/lib through their RPATH, so the
XCOMM system's X11R4 libX11.so and friends in /usr/lib are left alone.
#define HasSharedLibraries YES
#define ForceNormalLib YES
#define SeparateSharedCompile YES
#define SharedDataSeparation NO
#define PositionIndependentCFlags -fPIC
#define SharedLibraryLoadFlags -shared -L@ASV_SYSROOT@/usr/lib
#define ExtraLoadFlags -rpath=/usr/x11r6/lib -rpath-link=$(BUILDLIBDIR)
XCOMM No DT_NEEDED in our shared libraries, as in ASV's own: the system's
XCOMM dlopen/dlsym (in libc.so.1, which is also the dynamic linker) reads a
XCOMM library's NEEDED names with the wrong string table and faults once any
XCOMM loaded library has them. Clients name every library they use instead.
#define FixupLibReferences() XMULIB = $(XMULIBONLY) $(XTOOLLIB)
#define SharedX11Reqs /**/
#define SharedICEReqs /**/
#define SharedXextReqs /**/
#define SharedXtReqs /**/
#define SharedXmuReqs /**/
#define SharedXawReqs /**/
#define SharedSMReqs /**/
#define SharedXiReqs /**/
#define SharedXtstReqs /**/
#define SharedXieReqs /**/
#define SharedXpReqs /**/
#define SharedPexReqs /**/
#define SharedOldXReqs /**/
#define HasShm NO
XCOMM AMIX_RETURN_D0_TO_A0: every function returns its value in a0 as well as
XCOMM d0, for code that calls integer-returning functions through pointer-
XCOMM returning prototypes (the m68k SVR4 ABI keeps them apart; XView needs it)
#define CcCmd env AMIX_SYSROOT=@ASV_SYSROOT@ AMIX_RETURN_D0_TO_A0=1 @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-gcc
#define ServerCcCmd env AMIX_SYSROOT=@ASV_SYSROOT@ AMIX_RETURN_D0_TO_A0=1 @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-gcc
#define DefaultCCOptions /**/
#define DefaultCDebugFlags -O
#define OptimizedCDebugFlags -O
#define CppCmd /usr/bin/cpp
#define RawCppCmd /usr/bin/cpp -undef
#define PreProcessCmd /usr/bin/cpp
#define UnixCpp YES
#define XawI18nDefines -DUSE_XWCHAR_STRING -DUSE_XMBTOWC

#define ArCmdBase @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-ar
#define RanlibCmd @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-ranlib
#define AsCmd @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-as
#define LdCmd @ASV_CROSS@/bin/m68k-cbm-sysv4-ld
#define LexCmd lex
#define MakeCmd make
#define YaccCmd yacc
#define DependCmd true
#define TermcapLibrary @ASV_SYSROOT@/usr/ccs/lib/libtermcap.a
#define LexLib	 @ASV_SYSROOT@/usr/ccs/lib/libl.a
#define CursesLibrary @ASV_SYSROOT@/usr/ccs/lib/libcurses.a

#define ConnectionFlags -DTCPCONN
#define HasSockets YES
#define NeedBerklib YES
#define SocketLibraries -lsocket -lsockhost -lnsl
XCOMM Linked into everything as plain objects (the cross wrapper puts archives
XCOMM before -l libraries, and shared libraries resolve them from the program):
XCOMM - libcextra.o: what ASV's libc.so.1 leaves to the static libc.a - setitimer,
XCOMM   sys_errlist, syscall, vfork, cfree, libm's fpset helpers - extracted by
XCOMM   build.sh with libcextra.py (following their own dependencies), ld -r;
XCOMM - asvcompat.o: the BSD names libsocket/libnsl call (select, syslog,
XCOMM   getgrent ...) that libc exports only as _abi_*;
XCOMM - asviob.o: stdio's stdin/stdout/stderr (see mksysroot.sh).
#define AsvLibcObjs $(TOP)/config/asv/libcextra.o
#define AsvFpsetObjs AsvLibcObjs $(TOP)/config/asv/asvcompat.o $(TOP)/config/asv/asviob.o
#define ExtraLibraries SocketLibraries AsvFpsetObjs
#define ServerExtraSysLibs -lgen -lm
#define HasSecureRPC NO
#define HasPutenv YES
#define HasStrcasecmp NO
#ifdef StrcasecmpDefines
#undef StrcasecmpDefines
#endif
#define StrcasecmpDefines -DNEED_STRCASECMP

#define BuildPexExt NO
#define BuildXIE NO
#define BuildLBX NO
#define BuildXKB NO
#define BuildXKBlib YES
#define BuildXInputExt NO
#define BuildDBE YES
#define BuildRECORD NO
#define BuildXCSecurity NO
#define BuildAppgroup NO
#define BuildFontServer NO
#define BuildSpeedo NO
#define BuildType1 NO
#define XAtwServer YES
XCOMM xdm's greeter linked in, not dlopen()ed as on other SVR4 systems
#define SharedLibXdmGreet NO
XCOMM the R6 fonts (PCF, built on the host by build.sh) first, then the
XCOMM system's X11R4 SNF fonts; the system's rgb database
#define DefaultFontPath /usr/x11r6/lib/X11/fonts/misc/,/usr/x11r6/lib/X11/fonts/75dpi/,/usr/x11r6/lib/X11/fonts/100dpi/,/usr/lib/X11/fonts/misc/,/usr/lib/X11/fonts/75dpi/,/usr/lib/X11/fonts/100dpi/
#define DefaultRGBDatabase /usr/lib/X11/rgb
#define XAmixServer NO
#define XprtServer NO
#define BuildXprint NO
#define XnestServer NO
#define XVirtualFramebufferServer NO

#include <sv4Lib.rules>
